      "id": "mycorner-08",
      "title": "Pause, Verify, Tell",
      "form": "practical procedure",
      "spokenWordCount": 279,
      "paragraphs": [
        "This is Pause, Verify, Tell, by the Finn Reading Game editorial team. It is practical procedure created for the Finn Reading Game.",
        "An urgent message tries to make speed feel safer than thought. The first repair is a pause.",
        "Do not send money, codes, account details, photographs of identification, or login information. Do not click a payment link supplied by the person making the request. A request can sound polite, frightening, official, or unusually generous and still be unsafe.",
        "Next, verify through a separate route. If the message appears to come from someone you know, contact that person using a phone number or account you already had. Do not rely on the number, link, or address inside the suspicious message. If the sender claims to represent an organization, find the organization's official contact information independently.",
        "Look for pressure tactics. Urgency, secrecy, a large promised reward, and an advance payment are meaningful when they appear together. A profile photograph, confident title, or long biography does not establish identity. Compare the claim with account history, dates, public schedules, and details that can be checked elsewhere.",
        "Then tell a trusted adult. Showing the complete message is more useful than retelling only its most dramatic sentence. Preserve the username, time, link, and any changes to the profile. Reporting can protect the recipient and help a platform investigate related accounts.",
        "Finally, remember that pausing is not rude and verification is not an accusation. A legitimate person can tolerate a safe check. The goal is not to win an argument with the sender or expose them personally. The goal is to prevent harm: pause, send nothing, verify another way, preserve the evidence, and tell someone."
      ],
