      "id": "mycorner-09",
      "title": "The Dog and the Shadow",
      "form": "fable with editorial introduction",
      "spokenWordCount": 275,
      "paragraphs": [
        "This is The Dog and the Shadow, by the Aesop and the Finn Reading Game editorial team. It is fable with editorial introduction created for the Finn Reading Game.",
        "This is one of Aesop's briefest warnings about an impossible bargain. A dog was crossing a plank over a stream with a piece of meat in his mouth. Looking down, he saw his reflection in the water. He believed it was another dog carrying an even larger piece.",
        "The dog wanted both. He opened his mouth to snatch the meat from the reflected dog. The real piece fell into the stream and vanished. The second piece had never existed outside the image.",
        "The fable works because the mistake is easy to recognize after it happens. The dog compares something real with something that only appears available. Desire shortens the distance between seeing and acting. He does not test whether a second dog is truly there, whether the second piece can be reached, or what he will risk by opening his mouth.",
        "An impossible promise in a message can create the same structure. A stranger may display wealth, status, or an enormous future reward, then request a smaller real payment now. The promised gain shines like the second piece in the water. The requested payment is the meat already being carried.",
        "The lesson is not that every ambitious offer is false or that wanting more is shameful. It is that a large promise should not erase a present fact. Before giving up something real, a person can pause, verify the offer independently, and ask what evidence exists beyond the reflection created by the person making the promise."
      ],
